the chief prosecutor of the  international criminal court is seeking  to open an investigation into the  philippine government's war on drugs  president rodrigo duterte's regime has  killed at least six thousand suspected  drug dealers  the icc claims this could amount to  crimes against  humanity the philippines is not a member  of the icc and duterte says that he will  not cooperate with the investigation  calling it politically motivated let's  get more we are joined by carlos conde  he is a senior philippines researcher at  the human rights watch  group and he joins us from manila  welcome to you carlos and thank you so  much for being on the program  talk with us a little bit more about the  war on drugs  in duterte's philippines  the war on drugs which started in 2016  when  the 30 became president has killed  thousands and thousands of filipinos  mostly poor filipinos across the  philippines and this has been  the one single human rights catastrophe  this country has ever seen  and so um you know hundreds and  thousands of families have been  suffering from the impact of the drug  war including children  and this has debilitated and devastated  their lives and so this  uh news today or last night about the  icc  requesting an investigation into these  killings is very definitely a welcome  news uh  for many of these uh victims of the drug  war and also  uh there's a lot of hope that this will  ultimately lead to accountability  how high can that hope possibly be  though carlos let me ask you because you  know as we've heard duterte says he  couldn't care less  about the court's investigation he's  also said that he will not  cooperate so realistically speaking how  far can the icc get  under those conditions you think  well uh the hope is that he this can get  as high as possible uh to the third  in fact in the request finding that uh  uh  prosecutor bensouda uh filed before the  icc pretrial pre-trial chamber  it was clear that he she uh made a  connection between  uh public officials police drug  officials and local government officials  who enforce  the the war on drugs to a connection to  the president himself so  uh i think the the icc uh investigators  are going to aim pretty high on this one  now  whether uh duterte will cooperate with  the uh  investigators um at this point actually  that does not  uh matter a lot a lot of these victims  because  he has said the same thing to uh uh the  special  rapporteur of the u.n on election  judicial killings and he has pretty much  um taken efforts to try to evade  accountability  uh but that this has not stopped the icc  from conducting its preliminary  examination and now it has not stopped  uh ex-prosecutor bensouda to request a  formal investigation where does the  public stand on all of this i'm just  curious because you know  duterte he he always defends his  crackdown claiming that he's only doing  what the people want him to do  well to a large degree uh the filipino  public still support him he's still  extremely popular  um his drug war policy if you talk to  people uh  his supporters are very very vocal about  supporting it so  i think uh the administration is  counting very much on this uh public  support but  on the other hand i think that this  decision by the icc  to really commence the process of  accountability  will uh hopefully that will swing some  uh uh perspectives toward uh toward  justice and toward accountability and to  really  uh force the government to end uh the  continuing  killings in the philippines uh related  to the drug war because even as we speak  the killings are still happening on  a regular basis and so i think that  this decision by the icc prosecutor will  help  convince uh some segment of the filipino  public to  to let accountability accountability  take its course  and it could amount to crimes against  humanity as the icc  attempts to get underway with this  investigation carlos conde senior  philippines researcher at human rights  watch thank you so much  you
 

